module lib/hf/md_onebody
  The one-electron integrals of Hartree-Fock, by the McMurchie-Davidson
  method: the overlap matrix S (how much two basis functions share), the
  kinetic energy T, and the attraction V between an electron and the nuclei.
  `H = T + V` is the part of the Fock matrix that does not depend on the
  other electrons, and S is what the SCF orthogonalizes against.

  McMurchie-Davidson writes the product of two Gaussians as a sum of Hermite
  Gaussians centered between them; the expansion coefficients (the E tables
  of lib/hf/hermite) depend only on the two exponents and the distance
  between the centers. So the tables are built once per pair of primitives
  and serve every component pair of the two shells. This is one of the
  integral families the integrals stage can pick; the water story runs it.

md1_grid  @a739afa5
  why   The Boys function values the nuclear attraction needs are read from
        a table built once per run; building it once here keeps it out of
        the loop over primitive pairs.
  shells  the shell pack
  lmax    the highest angular momentum of any shell
  s       the shell being read

md1_pair  @ec2d247f
  why   Everything the three integrals of a shell pair share (the E tables,
        the component factors, the pair's center P) is computed once per
        primitive pair and used for S, T and V together.
  how   For each pair of primitives: fill the E tables of the three axes,
        then for each component pair add the overlap (a product of three
        one-axis overlaps) and the kinetic energy (the right-hand Gaussian's
        second derivative, which is three overlaps with its power shifted by
        $-2, 0, +2$); then for each charged atom fill the Hermite integrals
        R at $P - C$ and add $\sum_{tuv} E_x(t) E_y(u) E_z(v) R(t, u, v)$ to V.
  atoms   the atoms pack: charge and position of each atom
  shells  the shell pack
  prims   the primitive pack: exponent and coefficient of each Gaussian
  cn      each primitive's contraction coefficient, scaled so the shell is
          normalized (from bas_contract)
  G       the Boys grid from md1_grid
  sa      the first shell of the pair (the rows of the block)
  sb      the second shell of the pair (the columns)
  la      $l_a$  the first shell's angular momentum
  lb      $l_b$  the second shell's angular momentum
  na      how many Cartesian components the first shell has
  nb      how many Cartesian components the second shell has
  PA      the powers $(i, j, k)$ of $x^i y^j z^k$ of each component of the first shell, `[na, 3]`
  PB      the same for the second shell, `[nb, 3]`
  ca      the atom the first shell sits on
  cb      the atom the second shell sits on
  NA      the normalization factor of each component of the first shell
  NB      the same for the second shell
  L       $l_a + l_b$, the highest Hermite order V needs
  lb2     $l_b + 2$: the E tables go two powers past $l_b$, because the
          kinetic energy shifts the right-hand power by 2
  ld      the stride of the right-hand power in a flat E table
  nt      the stride of the left-hand power, the number of Hermite orders kept
  EX      the E table along x for the current primitive pair, flat
  EY      the E table along y
  EZ      the E table along z
  D       $L + 1$, the side of the cube of Hermite integrals R
  RS      the Hermite integrals $R(t, u, v)$ for the current atom, already
          multiplied by $-Z (2 \pi / p)$, with scratch space after them
  F       the Boys function values $F_0 \ldots F_L$ at the current atom
  out     the block `[MD1_NMAT, na * nb]`: S, T and V for each component pair
  pa0     the first primitive row of the first shell
  pb0     the first primitive row of the second shell
  a       $a$  the exponent of the first primitive
  b       $b$  the exponent of the second primitive
  p       $p = a + b$, the exponent of their product
  c       the product of the two primitives' contraction coefficients
  sq      $\sqrt{\pi / p}$, which turns $E(i, j, 0)$ into a one-axis overlap
  Px      $P_x$  the center of the product Gaussian, x; `Py` and `Pz` likewise
  sx      the one-axis overlap along x for the current component pair
  tx      the one-axis kinetic energy integral along x
  w       the weight of the current component pair: contraction and
          normalization factors
  k       the column of the block that holds the current component pair
  Zc      the charge of the atom whose attraction is being added
  X       $P_x - C_x$, the pair center's offset from that atom along x
  acc     the sum over Hermite orders for one component pair and one atom
  Ax      the x coordinate of the first shell's atom
  Ay      the y coordinate of the first shell's atom
  Az      the z coordinate of the first shell's atom
  Bx      the x coordinate of the second shell's atom
  By      the y coordinate of the second shell's atom
  Bz      the z coordinate of the second shell's atom
  i       the component of the first shell
  j       the component of the second shell
  ip      the primitive of the first shell
  jp      the primitive of the second shell

  = c      a new primitive pair: its E tables along x, y and z are filled right after, and serve every component pair below
  = sq     the one-axis overlap of two s functions: $\sqrt{\pi / p}$ times $E(0, 0, 0)$
  = tx     the kinetic energy along x: the second derivative of the right-hand Gaussian, as overlaps with its power raised by 2 and kept
  = tx#2   and the term with its power lowered by 2, which exists only when that power is at least 2
  = X      the attraction to one atom starts from the pair center's offset from it: the Boys values at $p \lvert P - C \rvert^2$ and the Hermite integrals at $P - C$ follow, with the charge and $2 \pi / p$ folded in
  = acc#2  one term of $\sum_{tuv} E_x(t) E_y(u) E_z(v) R(t, u, v)$

md1_all  @db6533ae
  why   Computing each pair once, for `sa >= sb`, and writing it at both
        places halves the work and makes every matrix symmetric by
        construction.
  atoms   the atoms pack
  shells  the shell pack
  prims   the primitive pack
  n       the number of Cartesian basis functions
  cn      the normalized contraction coefficients
  G       the Boys grid
  M       the three matrices, `[MD1_NMAT, n, n]`: S, T and V
  sa      the first shell of the pair
  sb      the second shell, never after `sa`

md1_scatter  @f8cf17df
  M       the three matrices being filled
  B       one pair block from md1_pair
  shells  the shell pack
  sa      the pair's first shell
  sb      the pair's second shell
  na      the first shell's number of components
  nb      the second shell's number of components
  a0      the index of the first shell's first basis function
  b0      the index of the second shell's first basis function
  k       the matrix being filled: S, T or V
  i       the component of the first shell
  j       the component of the second shell

md1_pick  @5fbda183
  why   The integrals stage takes one matrix per member call; this builds
        all three and keeps the one asked for.
  atoms   the atoms pack
  shells  the shell pack
  prims   the primitive pack
  which   which matrix: `MD1_S`, `MD1_T` or `MD1_V`

